{
  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Eliminate most of the structural deficit within a Parliament; independent Office for Budget Responsibility; immediate £6bn savings and a one‑year public sector pay freeze; simplify and lower business taxes with an Office of Tax Simplification; Single Work Programme with sanctions for refusal and community work placements; NI holiday for first 10 hires; publish detailed government spending and contracts; place prudential bank supervision with the central bank; tougher crime measures (instant ASB sanctions, prison for knife crimes), Border Police Force and expanded prison capacity; real‑terms NHS funding growth, Cancer Drugs Fund, support for carers. TEXT_D: Raise the personal allowance to £10,000 funded by progressive taxation (align CGT with income tax; 1% levy on property >£2m); aim to halve the deficit by 2013–14; cap public‑sector pay rises at £400; scrap ID cards, reduce pre‑charge detention to 14 days, scrap control orders; presumption against short prison sentences and expand restorative justice; extensive decentralisation and proportional representation; oppose new nuclear; more permissive asylum/immigration measures (right to work, earned citizenship).",
  "reasoning": "The electorate’s high weight on proportionality and care with substantial authority is better matched by TEXT_E’s contribution‑focused welfare and fiscal discipline, tax simplification, conditionality in benefits, strong law‑and‑order stance, and protection of core health services. TEXT_D places greater emphasis on equality and civil liberties with less emphasis on authority and a less stringent fiscal consolidation, making it a looser fit to this profile.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
